    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act-sch-1-sec-44",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "44 Telecommunications (Consumer Protection and Service Standards) Amendment Act",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act#sch-1-sec-44"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act.pdf, p.48",
      "values:originalText": "(No. 2) 2000       No. 142, 2000\n\n(3) A draft standard marketing plan for an applicant for approval as a\ncompeting universal service provider for a universal service area in\nrespect of a contestable service obligation is a plan that sets out:\n\n(a) the equipment, goods or services that the applicant will\nsupply in fulfilment of that contestable service obligation, so\nfar as it relates to that area; and\n\n(b) the arrangements for supplying and marketing the equipment,\ngoods or services;\nbut does not deal with alternative telecommunications services.\n\n(4) A draft standard marketing plan that has been approved by the\nACA under section 13K, and that is in force, is an approved\nstandard marketing plan for the applicant or competing universal\nservice provider concerned.\n\n(5) Each draft or approved standard marketing plan may cover one or\nmore universal service areas in respect of one or more contestable\nservice obligations.",
      "skos:definition": "(No. 2) 2000       No. 142, 2000\n\n(3) A draft standard marketing plan for an applicant for approval as a\ncompeting universal service provider for a universal service area in\nrespect of a contestable service obligation is a plan that sets out:\n\n(a) the equipment, goods or services that the applicant will\nsupply in fulfilment of that contestable service obligation, so\nfar as it relates to that area; and\n\n(b) the arrangements for supplying and marketing the equipment,\ngoods or services;\nbut does not deal with alternative telecommunications services.\n\n(4) A draft standard marketing plan that has been approved by the\nACA under section 13K, and that is in force, is an approved\nstandard marketing plan for the applicant or competing universal\nservice provider concerned.\n\n(5) Each draft or approved standard marketing plan may cover one or\nmore universal service areas in respect of one or more contestable\nservice obligations.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act-sch-1-sec-44-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act-sch-1-sec-44-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act-sch-1-sec-44-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "979e11152bb97086837bd46c20185accb794ccfc420df3c8dfc17f4413c44d83"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act#sch-1-sec-44",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "44 Telecommunications (Consumer Protection and Service Standards) Amendment Act",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 48,
      "values:originalText": "(No. 2) 2000       No. 142, 2000\n\n(3) A draft standard marketing plan for an applicant for approval as a\ncompeting universal service provider for a universal service area in\nrespect of a contestable service obligation is a plan that sets out:\n\n(a) the equipment, goods or services that the applicant will\nsupply in fulfilment of that contestable service obligation, so\nfar as it relates to that area; and\n\n(b) the arrangements for supplying and marketing the equipment,\ngoods or services;\nbut does not deal with alternative telecommunications services.\n\n(4) A draft standard marketing plan that has been approved by the\nACA under section 13K, and that is in force, is an approved\nstandard marketing plan for the applicant or competing universal\nservice provider concerned.\n\n(5) Each draft or approved standard marketing plan may cover one or\nmore universal service areas in respect of one or more contestable\nservice obligations."
    },
