    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act-sch-1-sec-13g",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "13G Minister may determine requirements for drafts",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act#sch-1-sec-13g"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act.pdf, p.48",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Minister may determine in writing requirements for draft\npolicy statements and draft standard marketing plans of competing\nuniversal service providers.\n\n(2) These are some examples of requirements for draft standard\nmarketing plans:\n\n(a) timeframes for the supply of specified equipment, goods or\nservices;\n\n(b) performance standards relating to the fulfilment of the\nuniversal service obligation;\n\n(c) processes for advising persons about the availability, offer\nand supply of equipment, goods or services in the fulfilment\nof the universal service obligation, and the terms and\nconditions on which the equipment, goods or services are\noffered or supplied;\n\n(d) the form of a draft standard marketing plan.\n\n(3) A determination under subsection (1) is a disallowable instrument\nfor the purposes of section 46A of the Acts Interpretation Act 1901.\n\nNew Part 2 of the Telecommunications (Consumer Protection and Service Standards)\nAct 1999  Schedule 1\n\nTelecommunications (Consumer Protection and Service Standards) Amendment Act (No. 2) 2000\nNo. 142, 2000            45",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Minister may determine in writing requirements for draft\npolicy statements and draft standard marketing plans of competing\nuniversal service providers.\n\n(2) These are some examples of requirements for draft standard\nmarketing plans:\n\n(a) timeframes for the supply of specified equipment, goods or\nservices;\n\n(b) performance standards relating to the fulfilment of the\nuniversal service obligation;\n\n(c) processes for advising persons about the availability, offer\nand supply of equipment, goods or services in the fulfilment\nof the universal service obligation, and the terms and\nconditions on which the equipment, goods or services are\noffered or supplied;\n\n(d) the form of a draft standard marketing plan.\n\n(3) A determination under subsection (1) is a disallowable instrument\nfor the purposes of section 46A of the Acts Interpretation Act 1901.\n\nNew Part 2 of the Telecommunications (Consumer Protection and Service Standards)\nAct 1999  Schedule 1\n\nTelecommunications (Consumer Protection and Service Standards) Amendment Act (No. 2) 2000\nNo. 142, 2000            45",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-consumer-protection-and-service-standards-amendment-act-sch-1-sec-13g-ss-1"
        },
        {
